//      an otherwise-transparent canvas rather than across a whole
//      standalone rectangle. Six frame props now instead of three
//      (`glitchSquare1Frame1/2/3` for the first square's spot,
//      `glitchSquare2Frame1/2/3` for the second's) since each square
//      needs its OWN three frames baked at its OWN bbox — sharing one
//      frame image between both squares would make them always
//      flicker in lockstep instead of independently. The flicker and
//      shake mechanics themselves (rapid staggered on/off blips,
//      brief erratic `translate()`) are otherwise unchanged from
//      item 9.
//
// `z-index: 1` on `.education-glitch-frame` matters for the same
// reason `.content` needs it — see the `.halftone-spotlight` comment
// in Layout.astro: without it, the page-wide cursor-spotlight would
// paint over these and mute them depending on cursor position. Not
// gated behind `prefers-reduced-motion` — Firefox and Brave both
// report "reduce" by default for privacy reasons unrelated to the
// viewer's actual motion preference, which would otherwise silently
// hide this on a CV meant to be seen by whoever an employer happens to
// open it with. Each square's three frames only render if all three
// of ITS OWN props are supplied (a partial set for one square renders
// nothing for that square, rather than a broken one- or two-frame
// flicker; the other square is unaffected either way).
//
// Usage:
//   <EducationIslandSection
//     id="section-education"
//     eyebrow="Island 03"
//     title="Adipiscing Elit Sed"
//     text="Sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum..."
//     image="/images/education_island_ver3.webp"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/education_island_clouds_background.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame1="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_1.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame2="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_2.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame3="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_3.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame1="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_1.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame2="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_2.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame3="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_3.webp"
//   />

// New: every image prop below is now an imported src/images/ module
// (`ImageMetadata`) instead of a public/images/ URL string, and every
// <img> in this file that renders one of them now renders <Image />
// instead — see src/pages/index.astro's frontmatter note for why.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro:assets';

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  image: ImageMetadata;
  alt?: string;
  cloudsBackground?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare1Frame1?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare1Frame2?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare1Frame3?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare2Frame1?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare2Frame2?: ImageMetadata;
  glitchSquare2Frame3?: ImageMetadata;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  image,
  alt = "",
  cloudsBackground,
  glitchSquare1Frame1,
  glitchSquare1Frame2,
  glitchSquare1Frame3,
  glitchSquare2Frame1,
  glitchSquare2Frame2,
  glitchSquare2Frame3,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section class="education-section sky-chain halftone-hover" id={id}>
  <div class="education-visual">
    {cloudsBackground && (
      <div class="education-clouds-wrap" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="education-clouds-track-row">
          <div class="education-clouds-track">
            <Image src={cloudsBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <Image src={cloudsBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="education-clouds-fill"></div>
      </div>
    )}

    <div class="education-image-wrap">
      <Image src={image} alt={alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

      {glitchSquare1Frame1 && glitchSquare1Frame2 && glitchSquare1Frame3 && (
        <>
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-a" src={glitchSquare1Frame1} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-b" src={glitchSquare1Frame2} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-c" src={glitchSquare1Frame3} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </>
      )}
      {glitchSquare2Frame1 && glitchSquare2Frame2 && glitchSquare2Frame3 && (
        <>
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-a" src={glitchSquare2Frame1} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-b" src={glitchSquare2Frame2} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <Image class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-c" src={glitchSquare2Frame3} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </>
      )}
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {/* set:html here because this text is a real HTML bullet list
         (see src/i18n/ui.ts) -- <div>, not <p>, since a <ul> can't nest
         inside a <p>. */}
      <div class="body-text" set:html={text} />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .education-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* Flat color, kept from an earlier request — still the fallback
       behind the illustration's transparent gap (see the top-of-file
       comment) for the moment before `cloudsBackground` loads in, and
       for callers that don't pass one at all. */
    background: #40CAEC;
  }

  /* Positioning context for `.content`, `.education-image-wrap`, and
     `.education-clouds-wrap` (see the top-of-file "Structure"
     paragraph for the full reasoning) — otherwise a plain, unstyled
     block. Steps 18-19 briefly routed `.content`'s centering through
     CSS Grid here (first directly, then via a dedicated overlay
     wrapper); step 20 reverted both attempts after they broke the
     image, so this is back to the same plain `position: relative` it
     had from step 8 all the way through step 17. */
  .education-visual {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Looping clouds layer, behind everything else — see the dedicated
     top-of-file comment ("Clouds background") for the full history and
     mechanics. Same full-bleed breakout as `.education-image-wrap`
     below. Height is `64.5%` of `.education-visual` — measured
     directly off `education_island_ver3.webp`'s own alpha channel
     (see step 13's history entry), matching exactly where the
     courtyard floor turns opaque — so the wrap always reaches down to
     that line, at any breakpoint, without needing its own media query
     (see the top-of-file "Narrow-window" comment). `position: absolute`
     excludes it from `.education-visual`'s grid layout entirely (step
     18), the same way it always kept this element from contributing
     height back into `.education-visual` itself even before that
     step. A column flex container with two stacked
     children (step 13): the clouds themselves, sized to their own
     natural height only and never stretched or cropped (so step 10's
     tileable-seam fix stays intact), and a plain white fill below them
     that grows to soak up whatever's left of this `64.5%` box — see
     `.education-clouds-track-row` and `.education-clouds-fill` below.
     `overflow: hidden` clips the whole column back down to this box.
     Sits behind `.education-image-wrap` purely by DOM order — no
     `z-index` needed, see the top-of-file comment. */
  .education-clouds-wrap {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    height: 64.5%;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* Sized to its own content only (`flex: 0 0 auto`) — exactly as
     tall as the clouds artwork renders at, whatever that works out to
     at the current viewport width, never stretched to fill the rest
     of `.education-clouds-wrap`. Its own `overflow: hidden` clips the
     always-200%-wide scrolling track back down to one wrap-width. */
  .education-clouds-track-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Two copies of the same image, edge-to-edge, slid rightward on a
     continuous loop — see the top-of-file comment for exactly how the
     `-50% → 0%` keyframes below work out to "clouds drift left to
     right, seamlessly." */
  .education-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start; /* don't let images get flex-stretched vertically */
    width: 200%;
    animation: education-clouds-scroll 60s linear infinite;
  }

  .education-clouds-track img {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 50%;
    width: 50%;
    height: auto;   /* natural aspect ratio — full source width per copy, never cropped; see step 10's history entry */
    /* New: explicit `aspect-ratio` (cloudsBackground's own real size) —
       <Image /> defaults to `loading="lazy"`, and Chrome specifically
       flags a lazy image whose box isn't pinned by fixed width+height
       or a declared `aspect-ratio`; `width: 50%; height: auto;` alone
       doesn't count, so this states the real ratio directly. */
    aspect-ratio: 7679 / 4320;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Grows to fill whatever vertical space is left in
     `.education-clouds-wrap` once the clouds row above has taken what
     it needs (`flex: 1 1 auto`) — see step 13's history entry for why
     this is a separate box (rather than one shared background behind
     both the clouds row and this space) and why the wrap's total is
     `64.5%` specifically. `margin-top: -1vw` (step 14) pulls it up to
     deliberately overlap the bottom edge of `.education-clouds-track-
     row` by a small, fixed amount — since this element comes AFTER
     the track row in DOM order, its opaque white paints on top of
     that overlap, guaranteeing there's never a razor-thin gap for the
     flat `#40CAEC` behind everything to show through at the seam,
     regardless of sub-pixel rounding or which clouds image ends up
     passed in as `cloudsBackground`. See step 14's history entry. */
  .education-clouds-fill {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    margin-top: -1vw;
    background: #ffffff;
  }

  @keyframes education-clouds-scroll {
    from { transform: translateX(-50%); }
    to { transform: translateX(0%); }
  }

  /* Full-bleed break-out: spans the full viewport width no matter what
     this section's own left/right padding is — same trick as
     OtherIslandSection.astro (see the component-level comment above).
     `line-height: 0` avoids the couple of stray px an inline <img>
     otherwise leaves below itself.
       `z-index: 1` (step 22) is new — this box previously had none,
     since it never needed comparing against anything beyond its own
     siblings inside `.education-visual`, where plain DOM order already
     put it above `.education-clouds-wrap`. It's needed now that
     InBetweenSection2.astro sits directly after this section in
     index.astro and deliberately overlaps its own looping clouds
     background up into this section's bottom edge (see that
     component's top-of-file comment for the full request/reasoning) —
     without an explicit z-index here, plain DOM order would let THAT
     section's overlap paint over this image instead of behind it,
     since it comes later in the page. `z-index: 1` matches the exact
     convention Layout.astro's `.halftone-spotlight` comment already
     documents for every other family member's own foreground artwork
     (`.art-island`, `.computer-island`, etc.) — a positioned element
     with an explicit positive z-index always paints above a plain
     `z-index: 0`/`auto` sibling, regardless of DOM order, which is
     what keeps this image safely in front of InBetweenSection2's
     clouds in their overlap zone. */
  .education-image-wrap {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .education-image-wrap img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    /* New: explicit `aspect-ratio` for the base illustration (`image`
       prop) — same reasoning as `.education-clouds-track img` above.
       The more specific `.education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-
       frame` rule below already sets fixed `width: 100%; height: 100%`
       (both non-auto), so it doesn't need this and isn't affected by
       it — this only reaches the plain base image. */
    aspect-ratio: 2500 / 1800;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Glitch "burst" spots — v10 (see the top-of-file comment, glitch
     overlays history item 10). Back to the ORIGINAL small-square
     positions/sizes this feature started with: each `<img>` here is a
     full illustration-canvas-sized, mostly-transparent image (a
     grayscale noise texture baked into one small spot, matching
     `gray_square_1.webp`/`gray_square_2.webp`'s own alpha-channel
     bounds from this feature's very first version), so `inset: 0` +
     `object-fit: cover`/`object-position: center` below line it up
     pixel-for-pixel with the base illustration automatically, at
     every breakpoint — including the narrow-window crop, since it
     crops in lockstep with the base image rather than needing its own
     hand-measured CSS rectangle (that was the previous, `v9` "burst
     zone" approach, and the source of a real narrow-width overlap bug
     with `.content` — see item 9's entry for what happened and why
     this approach doesn't have that problem). Mostly `opacity: 0` (see
     `education-glitch-burst-flicker` below) — only one of a square's
     three frames is ever meant to be visible at a time, taking turns. */
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    opacity: 0;
    animation:
      education-glitch-burst-flicker var(--burst-duration) steps(1, end) infinite,
      education-glitch-burst-shake var(--burst-duration) steps(30, end) infinite;
  }

  /* Each square's three frames share one `--burst-duration` (so their
     flicker and shake stay in sync with EACH OTHER), but the two
     squares' durations differ from one another so the two squares'
     bursts drift in and out of phase — same "don't let independent
     things read as one synced thing" idea used everywhere else in
     this file, down to the bob-timing offsets in
     HobbyIslandSection.astro. */
  .education-glitch-frame-group-1 {
    --burst-duration: 6.3s;
  }
  .education-glitch-frame-group-2 {
    --burst-duration: 7.1s;
  }

  /* Frame `a` uses the base rule's default `animation-delay` (`0s` for
     both animations, unstated). Frames `b`/`c` get a slightly earlier
     negative delay on ONLY the first (flicker) animation in the list —
     the second value, `0s`, re-states the shake's delay so it stays
     identical across all three frames of a group (only one frame is
     ever opaque at a time, so it doesn't matter that all three "shake"
     on the same schedule — what you actually see is just whichever
     frame is currently visible rattling in place). This staggering is
     what turns three otherwise-identical on/off blips into a rapid
     a-then-b-then-c flicker instead of all three flashing in unison.
     Compound selectors (`.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-
     frame-b`, not just the bare `-b` class) for the same specificity
     reason documented at length elsewhere in this file's history: the
     base `.education-glitch-frame` rule above sets the `animation`
     SHORTHAND, which would reset a lower-specificity override's
     `animation-delay` back to `0s, 0s`. */
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-frame-b {
    animation-delay: -0.18s, 0s;
  }
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-frame-c {
    animation-delay: -0.36s, 0s;
  }

  /* Invisible for the vast majority of the loop, with two rapid on/off
     "blips" packed into the last ~10% of it. Combined with the three
     frames' staggered delays above, this chains into a rapid
     multi-flash burst (a-on, b-on, c-on, a-on again, briefly, ...)
     lasting well under half a second, then silence again for the rest
     of the multi-second cycle — the "burst" itself. `steps(1, end)`
     (not the jump-cut `steps(26, end)`/`steps(30, end)` used
     elsewhere in this file) is deliberate: with only two states (0 or
     1) there's nothing for extra steps to subdivide — one hard cut per
     keyframe boundary is all a plain `opacity: 0 → 1 → 0` needs. */
  @keyframes education-glitch-burst-flicker {
    0%   { opacity: 0; }
    84%  { opacity: 0; }
    85%  { opacity: 1; }
    87%  { opacity: 0; }
    89%  { opacity: 1; }
    91%  { opacity: 0; }
    100% { opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* A brief, erratic `translate()` shake, timed to the same ~84%-93%
     window the frame flicker above uses, so whichever frame is
     currently visible visibly rattles while it's flickering rather
     than flickering perfectly still in place — the same "shaky line"
     idea from the previous (small-square) version of this feature,
     carried over and applied here too. Since this now animates
     `transform` on the same full-canvas `<img>` the flicker's
     `opacity` animates (rather than a separate wrapping box, as the
     `v9` "burst zone" version used), a shift of a few px moves the
     WHOLE mostly-transparent canvas — but since only the small baked-
     in noise spot is ever visible, that spot itself still visibly
     shakes by the same few px, same as before. `steps(30, end)` for
     the same jump-cut reasoning as the rest of this file's glitch
     animations. */
  @keyframes education-glitch-burst-shake {
    0%   { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    84%  { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    93%  { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
  }

  /* Slightly above dead-center of `.education-visual`'s own box —
     plain `position: absolute` + `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`,
     the same proven mechanism this file used from step 4 all the way
     through step 17 (re-anchored to dead-center, `top: 50%; left:
     50%`, in step 20, then nudged up to `top: 42%` in step 21 per
     your follow-up — see the top-of-file "Positioning `.content`"
     section for that history). Steps 18-19 tried to reach a similar
     centered result via CSS Grid (first sharing a grid cell directly
     with `.education-image-wrap`, then via a dedicated `.education-
     content-overlay` wrapper) — both attempts caused real visible
     regressions you reported ("the education_island is completely at
     a wrong place", then "even worse"), so step 20 abandoned the grid
     approach entirely in favor of this simple absolute-positioning
     technique, which never once broke the image across 17 earlier
     iterations. `z-index: 2` paints it above `.education-image-wrap`'s
     plain `<img>` (which sets none) and above the page-wide cursor-
     spotlight, the same reason the glitch frames' own `z-index: 1`
     needs one — see the `.halftone-spotlight` comment in
     Layout.astro. */
  .content {
    position: absolute;
    top: 42%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Wider text column on big screens, per request. */
  @media (min-width: 1100px) {
    .content {
      width: min(50ch, 100%);
    }
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .body-text ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 0;
    list-style: disc;
  }

  .body-text li {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .body-text li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .body-text a {
    color: #1a1a1a;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: #999;
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
    transition: text-decoration-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .body-text a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  /* `1024px`, not the family's usual `640px` — see the top-of-file
     comment for why. `.content` stays overlaid (absolute, same
     top/left/transform as the default rule) at every width, including
     here (step 20) — only the image (and, per step 17, the clouds
     layer's width) change at this breakpoint. See the top-of-file
     comment ("Narrow-window") for the image-crop reasoning, and the
     dedicated comment on `.education-clouds-wrap` a little further
     down for the step-17 clouds-zoom addition. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    /* `min-height` + `aspect-ratio` together (step 16) — whichever of
       the two computes the TALLER box wins, per the CSS sizing spec.
       `aspect-ratio: 5000 / 3600` is this image's own exact ratio, so
       whenever a window in this range is proportionally wider than
       that (a landscape tablet, a short/wide browser window), THIS
       branch wins and the box grows to match the image's own shape
       exactly — `object-fit: cover` then has nothing left to crop in
       either direction, guaranteeing the top/bottom are never cut off,
       on any window shape. On a genuinely narrow/tall phone,
       `min-height: 75vh` wins instead, and `cover` crops the
       LEFT/RIGHT sides against that shorter-than-100vh box — tuned to
       show a real sliver of both buildings rather than cropping in
       tight enough to show only the courtyard gap. See the top-of-file
       "Narrow-window" comment (and step 16's history entry) for the
       full reasoning and the numbers behind both values — `75vh` in
       particular is the easiest single number to retune by feel if
       this still isn't quite the right amount of building to show. */
    .education-image-wrap {
      min-height: 75vh;
      aspect-ratio: 5000 / 3600;
    }

    .education-image-wrap img {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      object-fit: cover;
      object-position: center;
    }

    /* Step 17: the clouds "sitting too high" / leaving too much plain
       white below them on mobile, per this step's history entry.
       `.education-clouds-wrap` (see its own comment above) is
       otherwise always exactly `100vw` wide at every breakpoint, so
       its `height: auto` clouds image renders at its own natural,
       un-zoomed height. But `.education-image-wrap` right above this
       comment gets effectively ZOOMED IN on narrow/tall phones (the
       `min-height: 75vh` branch wins over `aspect-ratio: 5000/3600`,
       so `object-fit: cover` scales the image up until it's 75vh
       tall, THEN crops the sides down to 100vw — the image is
       rendered noticeably larger than its natural 100vw-wide size
       before that crop happens). The clouds layer wasn't getting the
       same treatment, so on a typical phone it stayed tiny relative
       to the now-zoomed buildings below it, occupying only a thin
       strip at the very top of the 64.5% band instead of reaching
       down toward the courtyard floor the way it does on wide
       desktop screens (where nothing is zoomed, so the clouds'
       natural height already nearly fills that band). Fixed by
       widening `.education-clouds-wrap` here to match the SAME zoom
       factor `.education-image-wrap` is undergoing, computed exactly
       rather than guessed: `max(75vh, 72vw)` recomputes the exact
       height `.education-image-wrap`'s own dual constraint above
       resolves to at any viewport size (`72vw` is `100vw` at this
       image's `3600/5000` ratio, i.e. the `aspect-ratio` branch's
       height when width is `100vw` — the same math the browser does
       internally for that rule), then `* 25 / 18` (`5000/3600`
       simplified) converts that box height back into the equivalent
       "un-cropped" width the browser would have scaled the image to
       before `object-fit: cover` cropped it down to `100vw` — i.e.
       exactly how much wider than `100vw` the buildings image is
       effectively being rendered at. Applying that identical width to
       the clouds wrap makes its `height: auto` image scale up by the
       same factor, so it reaches proportionally the same distance
       into the `64.5%` band as it does on desktop — "matches the
       perspective," per this step's request. `left: 50%` +
       `margin-left: <half the new width, negative>` re-centers it on
       the viewport for this new, wider-than-100vw size (the default
       `right: 50%` / `margin-right: -50vw` above would fight a
       non-100vw width, so both are neutralized here); the extra
       overflow bleeding past both viewport edges is caught by the
       same page-wide `overflow-x: hidden`/`clip` on `html` in
       Layout.astro that every other full-bleed element in this file
       already relies on (see the top-of-file comment), so nothing
       here needs its own horizontal scroll guard. Nothing below
       `.education-clouds-wrap` needs a matching override:
       `.education-clouds-track-row`, `.education-clouds-track`, and
       its two `<img>`s are all sized in percentages of this wrap (see
       their own comments above), so they inherit the wider box
       automatically, and the seamless loop mechanic (step 10) is
